<p>Stephen Hackett has a great, in-depth history of that time Virginia Tech <a href="https://512pixels.net/2026/11/va-tech-system-x-system-g/">built one of the world&#8217;s most powerful supercomputers out of Macs</a>—<em>twice</em>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Tech tore down the Power Mac version of System X in January 2004 and rebuilt it with Xserve G5s that summer. ECC memory was a big part of the reason. As <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20040724145729/http://www.tcf.vt.edu/systemX.html">the school put it</a>: “We needed to upgrade the system to something with error correcting code (ECC) RAM. The Power Macs did not support it and the XServes were coming.”</p>
<p>  The new array was made up of 1,100 dual 2.3 GHz Xserve G5 servers and six Xserve RAIDs. Those 2.3 GHz machines were custom-built by Apple for Virginia Tech; the public wouldn’t be able to buy one until January 2005. Each one had 4 GB of that sweet, sweet ECC RAM and an 80 GB Serial ATA drive.
</p></blockquote>
<p>I love the extra mile Stephen went with the research on this one. Featuring some never-before-seen photos!</p>
